## Lucky 15 vs Other Multiple Bets
A Lucky 15 consists of four selections combined into fifteen separate bets, distinguishing it from alternative multiple bet structures through its inclusion of **singles** and total wager count. The bet comprises four single bets, six **doubles**, four **trebles**, and one four-fold **accumulator**, ensuring returns even when only one selection wins.
A **Trixie** uses three selections to create four bets—three doubles and one treble—but excludes singles entirely. This structure requires at least two winning selections to generate returns. A **Yankee** matches the Lucky 15's four-selection framework but produces only eleven bets by omitting the four singles. The Patent, built from three selections, generates seven bets by including three singles, three doubles, and one treble.
William Hill offers all these bet types alongside **each way** variations, which double the total number of wagers by covering both win and place outcomes. A **Heinz** expands to six selections and 57 bets, excluding singles but incorporating all possible doubles, trebles, four-folds, five-folds, and one six-fold accumulator.
| Bet Type | Selections | Total Bets | Includes Singles |
|----------|-----------|------------|------------------|
| Lucky 15 | 4 | 15 | Yes |
| Trixie | 3 | 4 | No |
| Yankee | 4 | 11 | No |
| Patent | 3 | 7 | Yes |
| Heinz | 6 | 57 | No |
The structural differences determine minimum winning requirements and potential return profiles. Lucky 15 bets guarantee some return with one winner due to the singles component, while Trixie and Yankee structures demand multiple successful selections to avoid total loss.
