## Frequently Asked Questions
### How much does a £1 Lucky 15 bet cost in total?
A £1 Lucky 15 bet costs £15 in total, as the £1 stake applies to each of the 15 individual bets comprising the wager. The 15 bets consist of 4 singles, 6 doubles, 4 trebles, and 1 four-fold accumulator, all calculated from your 4 selections. If you select the each-way option, the cost doubles to £30, as this creates 30 separate bets covering both win and place outcomes for all combinations. Always verify your total stake before confirming the bet to avoid unexpected expenditure.
### Can I get my stake back with one winner on a Lucky 15?
William Hill offers a consolation that returns your stake at double the odds when exactly one of your four selections wins and the other three lose. This provides partial compensation but does not guarantee profit — the return depends on the winning selection's odds. If your single winner was priced at 2/1, you would receive £6 (£3 win return plus £3 consolation bonus) against a £15 total stake, resulting in a net loss of £9. The consolation reduces total loss exposure but does not eliminate it when only one selection succeeds.
### What returns do I get with 2 winners in a Lucky 15?
With two winning selections, you receive returns from 2 singles and 1 double, while the other 12 bets fail. The exact return depends on the odds of your two winners — for example, two 3/1 selections would return £4 from each single (£8 total) plus £16 from the double, giving £24 total returns against a £15 stake for £9 profit. No consolation applies with two winners, as that bonus only activates when exactly one selection wins. The remaining 4 trebles and the accumulator require at least three winners to pay out.
### Is a Lucky 15 or Yankee better for horse racing?
A Lucky 15 suits bettors who want protection against complete failure, as the 4 singles guarantee some return with just one winner. A Yankee costs less (11 bets versus 15) but requires at least two winning selections to generate any return, making it riskier when backing longshots. For horse racing with each-way options, the Lucky 15 provides better downside protection through singles that can return place money even when selections don't win. However, the Yankee delivers higher returns when multiple selections win, as your stake concentrates on the higher-value doubles, trebles, and accumulator rather than spreading across four low-return singles.
### Can I use the William Hill Lucky 15 calculator on mobile?
William Hill provides its Lucky 15 calculator through its mobile platform, accessible via web browser or the dedicated betting app for UK players. The mobile calculator accepts the same inputs as the desktop version — four selections with odds in decimal, fractional, or American format, plus your unit stake. The tool automatically computes total stake, potential returns for all winning scenarios, and profit after deduction. Mobile access proves particularly useful for horse racing punters constructing Lucky 15 bets while reviewing race cards and form on the move throughout the day.
### What place terms does William Hill offer for Lucky 15 bets?
Place terms vary by race type and field size rather than bet structure. William Hill typically offers 1/4 odds for places in races with fewer runners and 1/5 odds in larger fields, following standard UK racing conventions. These fractions apply to the win odds to calculate place returns — a 4/1 selection with 1/4 place terms pays 1/1 for a place finish. The number of paying places usually extends to the top 3 finishers in smaller fields and top 4 in races with 16 or more runners. Each-way Lucky 15 bets apply these same place terms across all 15 place bets, doubling the total wager count to 30.
### Why shouldn't I use Lucky 15 for odds-on selections?
Odds-on selections below 2/1 rarely justify the 15-bet stake commitment, as the potential returns from singles, doubles, and trebles fail to offset the £15 total cost when only partial success occurs. Even if all four odds-on selections win, the accumulator return may barely exceed your stake after accounting for the low odds multiplied together. Experienced bettors target minimum odds of 2/1 or higher per selection to ensure sufficient return potential across the 15 combinations. Short-priced favourites suit simpler bet structures like doubles or trebles rather than full-cover bets that dilute returns across numerous low-value combinations.
### How do dead heats affect Lucky 15 place returns?
Dead heat rules divide your place returns when multiple horses tie for qualifying positions in a race. William Hill applies standard dead heat reductions by dividing your stake by the number of horses involved in the tie, then calculating returns at full odds on that reduced stake. For example, a £1 place bet in a two-way dead heat for third place becomes a 50p bet at full place odds, halving your expected return. This reduction applies independently to each of the 15 place bets in an each-way Lucky 15, potentially affecting singles, doubles, trebles, and the accumulator if multiple selections encounter dead heats across different races.